From a5a28f81c34ff10ffe78485463c5986222ed61c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:54:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: content mapping v2.9 for a display of display
 management 2 or 3

The Dolby VSVDB of a display gives the version of Dolby Vision display
management it has (2 and up, in version 1 and 2 blocks). Every display
was sent all the extension blocks of the RPU, those of content mapping
v4.0 (levels 3, 8 to 11 and 254) too. A display of display management 4
reads them; one of 2 or 3 is older than content mapping v4.0:

- An RPU carries the two parts as two payloads, the v4.0 one after the
  other, where a reader of v2.9 does not look (FFmpeg 9.0.2,
  libavcodec/dovi_rpudec.c: parse_ext_blocks() with ver 1 for levels 1,
  2, 4, 5, 6 and 255, then with ver 2 for 3, 8, 9, 10, 11 and 254).
- ETSI GS CCM 001 V1.1.1 (2017), the display management metadata of the
  time of those displays, defines levels 1, 2 and 5; the others are
  reserved: "shall not be present in the bitstreams conforming to this
  version", and a display ignores them (6.2.2).
- The Dolby Vision driver of Amlogic (CoreELEC linux-amlogic, amdv.c)
  sends level 11 in the vendor InfoFrame only to a display whose block
  says display management 4 or later.

So a display of display management 2 or 3 is now sent the v2.9 part:
levels 1, 2, 4 and 5 (6 and 255 are never sent), the bytes a display of
4 is sent with the v4.0 blocks taken out. Nothing changes for a display
of 4 or later, for one whose version is not known, or for metadata
without a v4.0 part. Until now this concerned displays with a version 2
block of display management 3; the next commit also takes the 12-byte
version 1 blocks of display management 2 and 3.

The bridge takes the version from its context for each frame it prepares
(dvbridge_set_display_management(); it is not kept in the state, which a
commit puts back as its frame had it), and Kodi gives it at the start of
each native output, from the display's block
(CWinSystemGbm::DisplayDolbyVisionDmVersion()). The log says it once when
it applies, the first frame of a stream says "content mapping v4.0, sent
as v2.9 for display management 3", and the Dolby Vision page of the
player information has a row for it, "Content mapping sent".

Not seen on a display of display management 2 or 3: none is in reach.
Tested on the host with a serializer test (17 checks: what each version
is sent, byte for byte against the payload of before with the v4.0
blocks taken out; a version change while a frame waits; the level 5
block made again; a reset); against the bridge before this change 5 of
them fail.
